diagnosis_text:
I cannot confirm any network black hole or forwarding fault. End-to-end reachability between the reported endpoints is healthy:
- pc_0_0 -> pc_0_1 ping: 0% loss, ~1 ms RTT (including 1500-byte DF pings), so PMTUD is fine.
- Traceroute from pc_0_0 to 10.0.1.2 shows the path 10.0.0.1 (leaf_router_0_0) -> 172.16.0.4 (to spine_router_0_0) -> 172.16.0.7 (to leaf_router_0_1) -> 10.0.1.2. The super_spine_router_0 is not on the data path for this east-west flow.
- BGP control plane is stable and correct on all routers. Both spines learn and advertise 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24, and the super-spine has both prefixes installed via 172.16.0.1 (eth0). No RIB/FIB anomalies observed.
- Interface counters on super_spine_router_0 (eth0/eth1) show no errors or drops.
- iperf from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 sustains ~127–131 Mbit/s; one 1-second interval showed retransmissions, consistent with transient congestion, not persistent blackholing.

Affected component: none; data plane and FRR/BGP control plane are operating normally.
Mechanism: Traffic between racks follows leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1; the “top-most” box is not in the forwarding path here, so it cannot be the point of loss.
Localization: End hosts and their directly attached leaves/spine.
Root cause: No network fault found; the reported symptom is not reproducible and does not align with current forwarding paths.
Corrective fix: No network changes required. If file copies still fail, investigate host/application issues (NIC/driver, TCP offloads, disk I/O) and capture packets on pc_0_0/pc_0_1 during a failure to isolate end-host causes. 
